% ============================================================================
% ocean-observing-instruments — each common ocean-observing instrument and
% the ONE quantity it measures or detects (adj-facts-stdlib, OCEANOGRAPHY).
% ============================================================================
% A THIRD "observation and measurement" axis in the Major Gap ADJ-STDLIB-
% COVERAGE.md 5.1 names for K-8 science, after the already-shipped
% `chemistry/measuring-tools.adj` (lab tools) and
% `meteorology/weather-instruments.adj` (weather-observing instruments) —
% this one covers OCEAN-observing instruments:
% `ocean_instrument(instrument, quantity)`. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? ocean_instrument(tide_gauge, $Q)   % sea_level
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `ocean_instrument(instrument, quantity)`, so the lookup above is the
% ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the quantity WITH its
% source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on an instrument
% not one of the three the cited pages name. The query also runs
% BACKWARD — bind a quantity and recall the instrument that measures it:
%
%     ? ocean_instrument($I, underwater_sound)   % hydrophone
%
% UNLIKE `weather-instruments.adj` (six rows sharing ONE NOAA source page),
% this table's three rows each have a DIFFERENT NOAA source page — the ADJ
% table grammar (confirmed by reading `weather-instruments.adj` and
% `word-families.adj` before writing this file) carries only ONE table-level
% `source`/`locator`/`trust` block, so per this stdlib's established
% discipline (`word-families.adj`'s multi-family extensions), the table's
% own citation is the PRIMARY/first-listed source below, and each OTHER
% row's own distinct citation is documented here in prose:
%
%     instrument   quantity            source page                                    quote
%     ----------   -----------------   ---------------------------------------------   -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
%     tide_gauge   sea_level           oceanservice.noaa.gov/facts/tide-gauge.html      "a tide gauge measures changes in sea level relative to a datum (a height reference)"
%     hydrophone   underwater_sound    oceanservice.noaa.gov/facts/hydrophone.html      "A hydrophone is an underwater device that detects and records ocean sounds from all directions" (elsewhere: "measure ocean sounds with great precision")
%     sonar        distance_to_object  oceanservice.noaa.gov/facts/sonar.html           determines "the range and orientation of the object" by measuring the time between an emitted sound pulse and its echo
%
% All three citations WebFetch-verified before writing. `trust authoritative`
% for all three — every row's own source page is a primary U.S. government
% (NOAA, .gov) source, the same tier `weather-instruments.adj`'s NOAA source
% earned. An instrument not one of these three — a CTD, a buoy, an ocean
% glider — has no row, so a recall on it ABSTAINS rather than inventing a
% quantity (a CTD in particular measures MULTIPLE quantities at once —
% conductivity, temperature, and depth — which does not fit this table's
% one-instrument-one-quantity shape, so it is deliberately excluded rather
% than force-picking one of its several measured quantities). (See
% ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table ocean_instrument {
    columns instrument, quantity

    row (tide_gauge, sea_level)
    row (hydrophone, underwater_sound)
    row (sonar, distance_to_object)

    source "a tide gauge measures changes in sea level relative to a datum (a height reference)"
    locator "https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/facts/tide-gauge.html"
    trust authoritative
}
